(a) Land disturbance activities which disturb greater than one (1) acre shall require a Storm Water Pollution Prevention Plan (SWPPP) as prescribed in the guide. The SWPPP must be available on-site and stored electronically and readily accessible to city staff.

(b) The SWPPP shall be prepared by a design professional as defined in Section 52.40.

(c) The SWPPP shall be modified by a design professional as required in the General Permit. Any modification of a SWPPP shall meet the requirements of this section.

(d) A SWPPP shall include the following:

(1) All requirements as outlined in the Guide;

(2) Compliance with all current minimum mandatory requirements for a SWPPP promulgated by the Minnesota Pollution Control Agency in connection with the General Stormwater Permit. The issuing authority may develop policies modifying these requirements for sites as outlined in the Guide;

(3) Utilization of an interactive website or other portal provided by the City of Minneapolis to retain SWPPP documents, document weekly SWPPP inspections, and record all land disturbance activity, sediment/erosion control practices, and good housekeeping practices;

(4) A signed and dated certification by the person preparing the SWPPP that the SWPPP complies with all requirements of this section; and

(5) Other requirements or requests as seen fit by the issuing authority during review.

(Ord. No. 2024-031, § 1, 9-5-24)

The permittee must inspect and maintain all erosion and sediment control best management practices (BMPs) to ensure integrity and effectiveness. The permittee must repair, replace, or supplement all nonfunctional BMPs with functional BMPs by the end of the next business day after discovery. Permittees may request additional time if field conditions prevent access to the area.

(1) Initial inspection. The permittee shall notify the issuing authority when initial erosion and sediment control measures are installed. No land disturbance activities shall begin prior to approval from the issuing authority that all pre-construction erosion and sediment control measures are correctly installed. Sites with an erosion and sediment control (ESC) Plan or storm water pollution prevent plan (SWPPP) shall demonstrate that pre-construction erosion and sediment control measures are in line with their approved plans.

(2) Routine inspection. The permittee shall ensure the entire construction site is inspected on a regular schedule and that BMPs on the site are maintained and functioning. The permittee shall inspect the construction site at least once every seven (7) days during active construction and within twenty-four (24) hours after a rainfall event greater than one-half (1/2) inch in twenty-four (24) hours. A written record must be kept of each inspection. The inspection frequency may be reduced to once per month on areas of the site with permanent cover. Inspections may cease during frozen conditions when construction has stopped but must resume within twenty-four (24) hours of runoff or if construction begins again. Any deficiencies shall be noted in a report and signed by the person performing the inspection.

a. Applicants must record and retain records of all inspections and maintenance activities within twenty-four (24) hours of being conducted. The inspection report shall include the following minimum information:

1. The date and time of the inspection;

2. The name of the person(s) conducting the inspection;

3. The findings of the inspection, including the specific location where corrective actions are needed;

4. Any corrective actions taken, including dates, times, and the party completing the maintenance activities;

5. The date of all rainfall events greater than one-half (1/2) inch in a twenty-four (24) hour period;

6. Any observed discharges during the inspection, with photographs, locations, and a description of the discharge; and

7. Any amendments to the SWPPP proposed as a result of the inspection.

b. The issuing authority may also perform inspections of the site to verify compliance with the ESC Plan or SWPPP.

c. Should it be found that the control methods are ineffective or not being maintained properly, the issuing authority may take enforcement actions described within this chapter.

(3) Final inspection. The applicant or representative shall notify the issuing authority and request a final inspection when the land disturbing operations are completed and the site is stabilized. The site shall be considered stabilized when all the following conditions are met:

a. All soil disturbing activities have been completed;

b. All drainage facilities and their protective devices have been installed;

c. Perennial vegetation or permanent cover covers all areas that have been disturbed. Perennial vegetation shall be considered established and completed for stabilization when it has established a healthy and growing stand with a uniform cover of at least seventy percent (70%) of the expected final vegetative growth density;

d. The permanent storm water management system has been constructed and is operational;

e. Sediment has been removed from basins, ditches, and other conveyance systems;

f. Temporary BMPs have been removed;

g. Any impacts to adjacent properties or other areas disturbed or impacted as a result are corrected; and

h. All site noncompliance issues have been resolved.

(Ord. No. 2024-031, § 1, 9-5-24)

