(a) Basis of bill. Utility billing shall base the water and sanitary sewer charges upon water meter readings, except as provided in sections 505.70 and 505.80. Charges authorized by state law, the city charter, or Minneapolis Code of Ordinances may be added to the utility bill. Other charges agreed to by the city and the customer may also be added to the utility bill.

(b) Billing information. An initial bill shall state clearly:

(1) The beginning and ending water meter volumetric reading of the current billing period;

(2) The dates of the water meter readings;

(3) Whether the charges are based on actual or estimated consumption;

(4) The date by which payment in full must be received to prevent the account from becoming delinquent;

(5) Any connection or reconnection charge;

(6) Any charge remaining unpaid;

(7) Any new charge for utility service during the current billing period;

(8) The total balance on the date of preparation of the bill; and

(9) The net amount due.

(Ord. No. 2022-024, § 2, 5-26-22)

(a) Notice. If a bill payer claims inability to pay a bill in full, utility billing shall inform the bill payer of utility billing's policies with respect to deferred payment agreements.

(b) Deferred payment agreements. Utility billing shall make a deferred payment agreement available to any qualifying bill payer. In deciding whether to offer a bill payer a deferred payment agreement and in determining the length of any such agreement, utility billing shall consider:

(1) The size of the account;

(2) The bill payer's ability to pay;

(3) The bill payer's payment history;

(4) The amount of time the debt has been outstanding;

(5) The reasons why the debt has been outstanding; and

(6) Other relevant factors concerning the circumstances of the bill payer.

(c) Terms of agreement. If utility billing and the bill payer decide to enter into a deferred payment agreement, the agreement shall provide for payment of future bills as they fall due.

(Ord. No. 2022-024, § 2, 5-26-22)

(a) Complaints. A bill payer, customer, or applicant may notify utility billing of any complaint or dispute in writing, by telephone, or in person within thirty (30) days of the due date of the bill being disputed. Utility billing shall investigate the complaint promptly and shall make a substantial effort to resolve it. A complaint not resolved may proceed to a formal complaint if it satisfies the requirements listed in this section for formal complaints.

(b) Formal complaints. Timely complaints received under (a) above and not resolved shall be placed in writing by the applicant, customer, or bill payer within forty-five (45) days after the due date of the bill in dispute. The complaint is deemed waived if received beyond the time limit. The formal complaint shall state the specific item(s) disputed on the bill, and reasons for the dispute. Utility billing shall investigate the complaint promptly and shall notify the bill payer, customer, or applicant of the results of its investigation and of its decision, in writing, within fourteen (14) days of receipt of the complaint.

(c) Appeal of formal complaint decision. On a formal complaint, the written decision of utility billing shall include the following information:

(1) Decision made by utility billing and the amount due;

(2) Bill payer, customer, or applicant's right to appeal the decision to an impartial hearing officer as provided in section 505.130;

(3) Appeals must be in writing and sent to the address provided in utility billing's written decision;

(4) Appeals must be received by the city within ten (10) calendar days of the postmark date of utility billing's written decision;

(5) Bill payer, customer, or applicant is entitled to only one (1) appeal hearing on the same dispute; and

(6) Decision of the hearing officer on an appeal may result in an assessment against the property with no further hearing.

(Ord. No. 2022-024, § 2, 5-26-22)

(a) Shut off notice. Except as provided in this chapter, the department shall not shut off water service unless utility billing sends a written notice to the bill payer, by first class mail or by personal service, at least ten (10) calendar days prior to the date of the proposed shut off. Utility billing shall keep an accurate record of the date of mailing or personal service of a shut off notice.

(b) Shut off; form of notice. A shut off notice shall contain the following information:

(1) The name and address of the bill payer if known to the department;

(2) A clear and concise statement of reasons for the proposed shut off;

(3) The date on or after which service will be shut off unless the bill payer acts to prevent it;

(4) A description of the appropriate action the bill payer must take to prevent shut off;

(5) The bill payer's right to dispute the shut off before an impartial hearing officer following the procedures pursuant to this chapter and that no water or sewer service shall be shut off while there is a complaint of fact under investigation or an appeal pursuant to this chapter; and

(6) The statement that if service is shut off, the bill payer shall be required to make payment of the delinquent account and other charges as provided in section 505.150, or make payment arrangements as provided in section 505.110, as a condition of restoration of service.

(Ord. No. 2022-024, § 2, 5-26-22)

(a) All water, stormwater, sewer, solid waste, recycling, water service line and sewer lateral repairs, and other charges that have been added to the utility bill and have not been paid within twenty (20) days after the bill has been sent shall be considered delinquent and may thereafter be assessed against the property served. Notice of the proposed assessment amount will be mailed to the taxpayer of record. The charges will be certified to the Hennepin County Auditor for collection, as other taxes are collected.

(b) If a taxpayer has not had an administrative hearing under Section 505.130 on the same disputed charge(s), an assessment hearing will be conducted. Not less than fourteen (14) days prior to the hearing, the department will mail notice to the bill payer. Such notice shall state:

(1) The date, time, and place of the special assessment hearing;

(2) The amount of money past due on the account;

(3) The bill payer must file written objections to the assessment no later than three (3) days prior to the hearing;

(4) The bill payer must indicate intentions of attendance prior to the hearing;

(5) City council will approve the special assessments at annual adoption of the assessment roll; and

(6) The bill payer may appeal the assessment to the district court within thirty (30) days after the adoption of the assessment by the city council.

(c) Notice of the hearing officer's decision will be mailed promptly to the bill payer following the hearing. It will include the hearing officer's decision, the amount due, and the statements of subsections (b)(5) and (b)(6) of this section.

(d) The amount of the assessment may include an administrative fee not to exceed ten (10) percent of the assessed amount and shall bear interest at such amount, which shall be set annually by the city's finance officer and shall reflect the current interest rate environment, the term of the loan, lost investment earnings, and administrative costs to the city. Such an assessment shall be collected as a special assessment payable in one (1) sum unless the city council, upon approval of such special assessment, extends the time for the payment thereof over such period of years as it may determine as authorized by Minn. Stat. § 435.17.

(Ord. No. 2022-024, § 2, 5-26-22; Ord. No. 2025-014, § 1, 2-27-25)

When the owner or agent of the owner of any premises has been served with an order by the health department to make sewer and water connections, and such owner or agent shall, for financial reasons, be unable to comply with such order within a period of thirty (30) days from the receipt thereof, such owner or agent may file a sworn statement with the commissioner of health. The sworn statement shall be made upon a form furnished by the commissioner of health and shall:

(1) Recite the reasons for the inability to finance the cost of making such connections;

(2) Recite whether, in the opinion of such owner or agent, the premises are likely to become a nuisance for the lack of sewer and water service;

(3) Authorize the city to enter upon the premises and make such connections and consent to the levying of a special assessment therefor; and

(4) Release the city from any claims or damages arising out of the making of such installation.

(Ord. No. 2022-024, § 3, 5-26-22)

Each year, the division shall submit complete budget estimates to the city's budget authority showing in full:

(1) All contemplated operations, improvements, and expenses of the division for the succeeding fiscal year;

